    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "arr = [4, 2, 1, 3]                    ->  [[1,2], [2,3], [3,4]]\narr = [1, 3, 6, 10, 15]               ->  [[1,3]]\narr = [3, 8, -10, 23, 19, -4, -14, 27] ->  [[-14,-10], [19,23], [23,27]]"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea: the closest pair is always adjacent once sorted"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In sorted order, if `a < b < c`, then `c − a` is bigger than both `b − a` and `c − b`. So a pair that isn't adjacent can never be the closest — some value in between is closer to each of them. That leaves only the **n − 1 gaps between neighbours**: find the smallest, then collect every neighbouring pair with that gap. Because we walk in sorted order, the pairs come out already in the required order."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Brute force: every pair"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
